## Introduction
The rapid growth of global energy consumption has led to many studies on green, clean, and renewable energy conversion systems such as solar cells and water splitting. Of these, electrochemical water splitting is one of the most promising strategies for producing clean fuels from renewable sources [1-5]. However, the sluggish kinetics of the anode oxygen evolution reaction (OER) largely hinders the realization of complete water splitting systems [6,7]. To date, noble-metal based catalysts such as $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ are the most commonly used OER electrocatalysts, but their largescale applications are impeded due to limited reserves, uneven distribution, and poor stability [8]. Recently, increasing attention has been focused on improving the performance of inexpensive electrocatalysts to replace precious metal-based catalysts.
In this respect, the construction of effective electrocatalysts from earth-abundant and non-noble metal elements has become an active research area. Many low-cost 3d transition metal(oxy)- hydroxides, sulfides, phosphides, oxides, and selenides have been developed as potential  electrocatalysts [9,10]. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted wide attention in many fields due to their diverse structures, large specific surface area, and high porosity. In particular, the electrochemical activity of MOFderived catalyst systems has been improved because of the increased number of exposed active sites [1l-l4]. However, most of previously reported MOF-derived materials commonly have very simple composites such as single-component porous or hollow structures stemming from high-temperature calcination. These seriously damage the unique MOF structures and reduce the accessibility of unsaturated metal sites. Therefore, non-calcined MOF-derived materials with diverse metallic, active sites are urgently needed.
Ion exchange is a feasible pathway for non-calcined MOFderived materials with diverse metallic active sites. Effectively introducing two or more metal ions into the MOF precursor is useful for synthesizing the targeted functional materials. It can also facilitate the transformation of nanomaterials' structure and composition. Controllable conversion of simple MOF crystals into complex nanomaterials via an ion exchange reaction can not only adjust both structural and chemical composition but also improve the chemical stability of the MOF materials themselves [15].In recent years, Prussian blue analogues (PBAs) have been described as a new energy-related material with low cost, convenient preparation, and open structure. They have broad application prospects in electrochemical energy-related fields such as batteries, supercapacitors, and oxygen reduction reactions [l6-18]. However, most researchers have focused on the use of Prussian blue and its analogues as promising templates or precursors by subsequent calcination to produce various active OER materials instead of directly applying them. For instance, Lou's group synthesized Ni-Co mixed oxide nanocages consisting of pyramidlike walls via cubic PBA-NiCo $\mathrm{(Ni_{3}[C o(C N)_{6}]_{2}{\cdot}\it{x H_{2}O)}}$ as a precursor [19]. Paik et al. also used PBA-CoCo $\mathrm{(Co_{3}[C o(C N)_{6}]_{2})}$ microcubes as templates to synthesize $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ microframes for OER via ammonia etching and annealing in air [20]. Kim et al. also reported $\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}$ nano-boxes for efficient OER by calcining PBA-CoCo nanocubes with selenium powder [2l]. Nevertheless, the active surface area and electron transport capacity of MOFderived PBAs can be improved via their diverse compositions and large specific surface area by anion exchange. This suggests that the complex PBAs can serve as effective water oxidation catalysts without calcination.
Herein, we report a feasible MOF hybrid-assisted approach to synthesize Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA (ZIF: zeolitic imidazolate frameworks) core-shell composites at room temperature as an efficient electrocatalyst for OER. This MOF-derived PBA has a superior electrocatalytic activity of OER with a small overpotential of $270~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and a low Tafel slope of $78~\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ These values are superior to the advanced $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ -based noble metal catalysts. In application to a water splitting device, this material delivers $1.62\mathrm{~V~}$ to $10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . We then explored the origin of Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA activity at the atomic level by Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), and other characteristics. The results showed that the highly active amorphous hydroxide was transformed in situ from Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA during the electrocatalytic reaction and acted as the main active substance to remarkably promote the OER process.
## 2 Experimental section
## 2.1 Materials
Synthesis of ${\bf C o/M n–Z I F}$ ：All purchased chemicals were used directly without further processing. In a typical synthesis, $50\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{MnCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $249~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ were dissolved in $25~\mathrm{\mL}$ of methanol solution under ultrasound conditions. Similarly, $492\ \mathrm{mg}$ of 2-methylimidazole (2-MI)was solubilized in $25~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol solution. Then, two solutions were quickly mixed under stirring and alowed to rest at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $35\mathrm{h}$ Finally, the purple precipitate $(\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Mn}{-}Z\mathrm{IF})$ was separated by centrifugation,washed with ethanol several times, and dried under a vacuum oven at $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$
Synthesis of $\mathbf{Co/Mn{\mathrm{-}}Z I F@F e{\mathrm{-}}C o{\mathrm{-}}M{\mathbf{n}}}$ PBA.The $40~\mathrm{\mg}$ of ${\mathrm{Co/Mn}}$ -ZIF powder was well dispersed in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol with ultrasonication. The $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{K}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe(CN)}_{6}]$ was then dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to form a homogeneous solution. The ${\mathrm{K}}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe(CN)}_{6}]$ aqueous solution and the ${\mathrm{Co/Mn}}$ -ZIF solution were then mixed with stirring, followed by continuous stirring at room temperature for $2.5\ \mathrm{h},$ and the product (Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA）was collected by centrifugation and washed several times with water and ethanol.
Synthesis of Fe-Co-Mn PBA. Here, $40~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{{Co/Mn}}$ -ZIF powder was dispersed ultrasonically in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol and then heated to $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ The $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{K}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe(CN)}_{6}]$ and $0.1~\mathrm{mL}$ of acetic acid aqueous $(0.1\ \mathrm{M})$ were mixed in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of distilled water to form a transparent solution. The mixed aqueous solution was then quickly poured into the ${\mathrm{Co/Mn}}$ -ZIF solution and stirred for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . The mixture (Fe-Co-Mn PBA) was centrifuged, washed with water and ethanol several times, and then dried in a vacuum oven at $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$
## 2.2 Characterization
The microstructure and morphology of the prepared catalyst were investigated by a Germany Zeiss SUPRA40 field emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM) at an operating voltage of $15~\mathrm{kV}$ and JEOL-10oCX high resolution transmisson electron microscopy (HRTEM) at an operating voltage of $150\ \mathrm{kV}.$ The crystal structure of the samples was characterized by Bruker D8 advanced X-ray diffraction (XRD) instrument. XPS measurements used a PHI 500oC ESCA system. The pore structure was analyzed with $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption measurements on a
Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surfaceareaanalyzer (Quantachrome Instruments QuadraSorb SI4), and the pore size distribution was obtained via the Barrett-Joyner-Halenda (BJH) model.
## 2.3 Electrochemical measurements
Electrochemical measurements were conducted on a CHI 760E workstation (CH Instruments, Chenhua, Shanghai, China) equipped with a rotating disk electrode (RDE) in a standard threeelectrode cell. A glassy carbon (GC) electrode ( $5\mathrm{mm}$ in diameter), a graphite rod, and a saturated calomel electrode (SCE) were used as the working electrode, counter electrode, and reference electrode, respectively. The potential recorded was converted to the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) according to the Nernst equation $E_{\mathrm{RHE}}=E_{\mathrm{SCE}}+0.059\times\mathrm{pH}+0.241$ . To prepare uniform sample ink, $2.0~\mathrm{mg}$ of electrocatalyst was ultrasonically dispersed in $485~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of isopropanol, followed by the addition of $15~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ $5.0~\mathrm{wt.\%}$ Nafion solution for continuous ultrasonic dispersion for another $30~\mathrm{min}$ .Next, $40~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of ink was loaded onto the prepolished RDE surface. The loading amount was calculated to be $\overline{{0.8153}}\quad\mathrm{mg{\cdot}c m^{-2}}$ The RDE loading with $0.2038\quad\mathrm{mg{\cdot}c m^{-2}}$ commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ was obtained with the similar process. The OER polarization curves were recorded from $-0.1$ to $1.0\mathrm{V}$ vs.SCE with a scan rate of $5\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ at a rotation speed of $1{,}600~\mathrm{rpm}$ .The iRcompensation was performed via electrochemical impedance spectroscopy to correct for the compensated potential. The linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves were replotted as potential $(V)$ versus log current (logj) to get Tafel plots for quantification of the OER kinetic performance of the as-prepared catalysts. We fitted the linear part of the Tafel plots to obtain the Tafel slope $(b)$ according to the Tafel equation $(V=a+b{\cdot}\log j)$ .The stability tests were performed via chronoamperometry at a specific over potential $(\eta)$ $(j=10\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ , and cyclic voltammetry (CV) scans at a sweep rate of $50~\mathrm{~mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ Electrochemical double layer capacitance $\left(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)$ was calculated by CV curve at a non-Faraday potential range without polarization current. The $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ value was converted to the electrochemical surface area (ECSA) using Eq.(1)
$$
\mathrm{ECSA}=C_{\mathrm{dl}}/C_{\mathrm{S}}
$$
where $C_{\mathrm{{S}}}$ is the specific capacitance with $0.040~\mathrm{mF}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ in $1\mathrm{M}$ of KOH electrolyte [22,23]. Typically, CV curves were recorded at various scan rates $(20,40,60,80,100\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}s^{-1}$ etc.）in 0.1 to $0.2\mathrm{V}$ vs. SCE range. The water splitting device used carbon cloth $(1.5\mathrm{cm}\times1.5\mathrm{cm})$ as the electrode. Gas chromatography was used to determine the amount of oxygen produced via water splitting device. The carrier gas was nitrogen, and the gas flow rate was $25\ \mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . The calculation formula of Faraday efficiency (FE) was according to Eq. (2) below
$$
\mathrm{FE}=n z F/(I\cdot t)\times100\%
$$
where $n$ is the number of moles of gas produced by electrolysis, $z$ is the number of electrons transferred, $F$ is the Faraday constant, $I$ is the applied constant current, and $t$ is the electrolysis time. All tests were performed in $1.0\mathrm{M}$ KOH solution.
## 3 Results and discussion
Figure 1 illustrates the overall synthetic process of core-shell Co/Mn-ZIF@Fe-Co-Mn PBA. First, a family of ${\mathrm{Co/Mn}}$ bimetallic MOFs (BMOF) was synthesized by a facile one-step solvothermal method at room temperature through the self-assembly of $\mathrm{Co^{2+}/M n^{2+}}$ with 2-methyimidazole in methanol solution. The samples were then used as a template to convert into a ${\mathrm{Co/Mn}}.$ ZIF@Fe-Co-Mn PBA core-shell composite via an anion exchange reaction with $\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]^{3-}}$ ions. Subsequently,Co/Mn-ZIF@Fe-CoMn PBA was converted into amorphous hydroxide in situ during the water oxidation process in alkaline medium, and the core-shell structure was well maintained.
Figure 3(a) shows the high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) image of a single Co/Mn-ZIF@Fe-Co-Mn PBA particle. The corresponding energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mapping images (Fig.3(b)) show that Co and Mn elements are uniformly distributed throughout the particles while the Fe element is only present in the outer shell. The differences of Fe,Co,and Mn contents in shells and cores are detected by EDS line scan (Fig. S4 in the ESM),which indicates that Fe decreases, but Co increases from edge to center of the catalyst particle. The outer shell structure was further characterized by HRTEM measurement. As shown in Fig.3(c), the typical lattice fringe of $0.253\mathrm{nm}$ is assigned to the (400) plane of Co-Fe PBA [25].Moreover, the selective area electron diffraction (SAED） image reveals the polycrystalline properties of Co-Fe PBA with lattice planes of (220) and (400) (Fig.3(d)) [26]. XRD analysis further indicates that two new peaks appear at $25.1^{\circ}$ and $35.5^{\circ}$ pointing to the (220) and (400) planes of Co-Fe PBA, respectively (Fig.3(e)), in addition to the diffraction peaks from Co/Mn-ZIF. These results suggest the formation of the core-shell structure: Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA. The XRD patterns of the single-shell Fe-Co-Mn PBA showed that the peak of Co/Mn-ZIF completely disappeared, which indicates the core was successfully removed in an acidic medium.
XPS measurement was used to probe the oxidation state of transition metals in Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA. The survey spectra (Fig.4(a)) show the presence of Fe, Co, Mn, C, N, and O elements in the nanocomposites. The Fe $\mathsf{2p}$ region in Fig.4(b) features a spin-orbit doublet at 708.3 and $721.3~\mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2},$ respectively. This indicates that the oxidation state of most iron species should be $+2$ The characteristic peaks at 710.9 and $724.8\:\mathrm{eV}$ are attributed to $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$ in PBAs, according to Ref. [27]. Therefore, divalent and trivalent irons are simultaneously present in Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA. The XPS spectra of $\mathrm{Co}~2\mathrm{p}$ are shown in Fig.4(c). The results demonstrate the co-existence of $\mathrm{Co^{2+}}$ (782.9 and $797.4\ \mathrm{eV})$ and $\mathrm{Co^{3+}}$ (781.2 and $795.3\mathrm{~\eV},$ by revealing a spin-orbit doublet for $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2},$ respectively [28]. O ls spectrum is deconvoluted into two peaks (Fig. 4(d)), and the peak at about $531\mathrm{eV}$ is attributed to the $-\mathrm{OH}$ group, whereas the peak at about $532.8~\mathrm{eV}$ indicates the presence of water molecules in the PBA structure. The Mn element is inconspicuous in the XPS spectra owing to its low contents, which is detected by the EDS scan line and inductively coupled plasma-optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES) measurements (Fig.S4 and Table Sl in the ESM). However, the Fe and Co oxidation states are further lifted by Mn doping through comparing their XPS spectra against those of the catalyst without Mn (Fig. S7 in the ESM).
Hierarchical micro/mesopore structure of the catalyst can expedite the mass transfer and the accessibility of active sites. Thus, nitrogen adsorption/desorption measurements were conducted to further research the porosity of the samples (Fig S2(a) in the ESM). The results show that, on the one hand, Fe-CoMn PBA revealed obviously lower BET surface area $(366~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ than that of Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA $\cdot862\mathrm{~\m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and
Co/Mn-ZIF $(1,450~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . It implies the loss of micropores in FeCo-Mn PBA without the core structure. On the other hand, the adsorption curve of CoMn-ZIF lacked the jump above 0.9 relative pressure revealed on the curve of Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA, indicating the deficiency of macro-pores in Co/Mn-ZIF. The pore size distribution further confirmed the co-existence of micropores and mesoporous in the Co/Mn-ZIF@Fe-Co-Mn PBA while revealing that CoMn-ZIF only possessed a single peak in the micro-porous area and Fe-Co-Mn PBA is a lack of specific peak of micro-pore before $2\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. S2(b) in the ESM).
We subsequently studied the electrochemical performance of these catalysts.First, the electrocatalytic activity of Co/Mn-ZIF@FeCo-Mn PBA was measured in 1 M KOH aqueous solution by LSV curveThe $\mathrm{Co/Mn\mathrm{-}Z I F@F e\mathrm{-}C o\mathrm{-}M n}$ PBArequires an overpotential of only around $270~\mathrm{\mV}$ to deliver the current densities of $10\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2};$ which is much lower than of ${\mathrm{Co/Mn–ZIF}}$ $330~\mathrm{mV})$ and Fe-Co-Mn PBA （ $310~\mathrm{mV}_{:}$ Fig. 5(a)). The ${\mathrm{Co/Mn}}.$ ZIF@Fe-Co-Mn PBA has better activity than the $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ -based precious metal catalysts $(320~\mathrm{mV})$ , which implies that it has highly applied potential.
Figure $5(\mathrm{b})$ shows that the Tafel slope for Co/Mn-ZIF@Fe-CoMn PBA is ${78}\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ which is smaller than that of $\mathrm{{Co/Mn}}$ -ZIF (88 mV-dec-), Fe-Co-Mn PBA (98 $3\mod5.\mathrm{dec^{-1}})$ ，and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ 0 $119\ \mathrm{mV}$ -dec-i). The data indicate the faster OER kinetics of Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA. The results above demonstrate that Fe-Co-Mn PBA has a smaller overpotential than that of ${\mathrm{Co/Mn}}.$ ZIF,while Co/Mn-ZIF shows better kinetic characteristics. The synergistic effect between the core and the shell endows the Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA with the best catalytic activity. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements show that Co/Mn-ZIF@Fe-Co-Mn PBA demonstrates a smaller semicircular radius than Co/Mn-ZIF, Fe-Co-Mn PBA, and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ This implies a benefit for expediting the electron transport process (Fig. 5(c)).
CV measurements were performed at different sweep speeds to investigate the ECSA of the samples. Faraday current wasn't observed in the potential range of 1.l to $1.2\mathrm{~V~}$ (Figs. 5(d)-5(f)).
According to the linear relationship between current density and scan rates (Fig. $5(\mathrm{g})$ ),the $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ values of the Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA, Fe-Co-Mn PBA, and $\mathrm{{Co/Mn}}$ -ZIF are calculated to be 42.63, 16.97, and $8.55~\mathrm{mF}$ ，respectively. We concluded that the ECSA of Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA is the largest. The results show that there are more active sites exposed in Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA, which greatly boosts the performance of the OER.
The durability of the $\mathrm{Co/Mn\mathrm{-}Z I F@F e\mathrm{-}C o\mathrm{-}M n}$ PBA catalyst during the OER process was also investigated. The current loss of Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA is negligible even after 1,00o cycles (Fig.5(h)). In addition, Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA was subjected to $10\mathrm{~h~}$ chronoamperometric test (inset) at designed $\eta$ $(j=10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ . The current of Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA increases within the first $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ and then slowly decreases. The total current enhancement is $58\%$ . These results demonstrate that the Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA catalyst is activated by the OER reaction, which is ascribed to the dynamic structural evolution of the catalyst structure [29-31]. The metal oxidation state evolution can also be detected by the metal-based redox peaks in the grey region of the CV curve during the catalyst precatalytic treatment (Fig. S5 in the ESM) [32].
Based on the fantastic OER performance of Co/Mn-ZIF@FeCo-Mn PBA, we prepared a water splitting device in which Co/Mn-ZIF@Fe-Co-Mn PBA was used as both anode and cathode with a loading amount of $4.7\mathrm{\mg{\cdot}c m^{-2}}$ The ${\mathrm{Co/Mn}}.$ ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA has a low potential of $1.62\mathrm{~V~}$ in the $1.0\ \mathrm{M}$ KOH solution when the current density reaches $10\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (Fig. 6(a)). The current-time curve was tested in Fig.6(b) to assess the stability of water splitting for Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA.No significant degradation of Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA was found within $10\mathrm{{h}}$ . Figure 6(c) shows the theoretical and measured amounts of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ .Within a certain electrolysis time, the amount of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ measured by gas chromatography is close to the theoretical value. Here, the average oxygen generation rate is $0.27\ \mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ and the Faraday efficiency is $92.6\%$ . The results above show that the as-prepared Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA can be used as a beneficial electrocatalyst for water splitting due to its excellent electrocatalytic performance.
The metallic active components in most first-row transition metal-based catalysts are likely to transform into corresponding hydroxides with enhanced activities during water oxidation in an alkaline medium [29]. Similar positive transformation was also desired but rarely introduced in surfaces of crystal catalytic materials [33]. To confirm this favorable evolution of our catalyst, we systematically analyzed it after a chronoamperometry test (signified as Co/Mn-ZIF@Fe-Co-Mn PBA-it). The TEM image (Fig.7(a)) shows that Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA retains the initial dodecahedral core-shell structure. TEM mapping images revealed that after $10\mathrm{{h}}$ of chronoamperometry, the catalyst has no obvious change in its component distribution (Figs. 7(b) and 7(d).
The in situ Raman spectra of Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA were measured under various potentials from $1.0{-}1.6\mathrm{V}$ vs.RHE. A rising peak at about $502~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be attributed to the $\mathrm{A}_{2\mathrm{u}}$ vibration peak of M-OH, which reveals the formation of hydroxides. Correspondingly, the decreasing peak at about 692 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ implies the partly collapse of the original ZIF structure [32]. The structural evolution can also be confirmed by the powder Xray diffraction pattern of Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA-it, which does not reveal any significant diffraction peaks. It further indicates that Co/Mn-ZIF@Fe-Co-Mn PBA was transformed into an amorphous structure (Fig. 7(c)).
The FTIR spectra of Co/Mn-ZIF $@$ Fe-Co-Mn PBA show the absorbance band at $2{,}078\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ which belongs to $\mathrm{C}{\equiv}\mathrm{N}$ connecting the $\mathrm{Co^{2+}}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ centers to form the polymeric structure of the PBA (Fig.S3 in the ESM) [34]. Furthermore, all of the characteristic peaks of the specific groups in the Co/Mn-ZIF phase at 690,755,990,1,135,1,300,1,410,and $1{,}588\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ completely disappear in the FTIR curve of the tested catalyst. These results indicate that the Co/Mn-ZIF@Fe-Co-Mn PBA definitely undergoes a structural and component evolution during water oxidation in an alkaline medium. XPS measurement after catalysis was conducted to detect the oxidation state and electronic environment changes (Fig.4). The results show that the $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Co^{2+}}$ orbits shifted to higher and lower binding energy, respectively, indicating their valence ascending. Meanwhile, the divalent peak of Fe $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ decreased, and the trivalent peak increased, also indicating Fe converted to a higher oxidation state. Similar evolutions can also be observed in the XPS spectra of ${\mathrm{Co/Mn}}$ -ZIF and Fe-Co-Mn PBA. These higher metal oxidation states would facilitate rapid charge transfer between the electrode and the electrolyte, which benefits the electrocatalytic reaction [35,
36]. Correspondingly, in O ls orbit, after the chronoamperometry test, the peak at $530.9~\mathrm{eV}$ is assigned to the metal-oxygen bonds. The two other peaks at 532.2 and $529~\mathrm{eV}$ correspond to surface -OH groups and adsorbed water molecules, respectively, which also confirm the hydration and hydroxylation during the water oxidization process [37]. These results fully prove that the metal species in Co/Mn-ZIF@Fe-Co-Mn PBA are converted into amorphous hydroxides during the alkaline medium electrolysis process. The materials act as active components and greatly improve the OER activity.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, we reported an innovative “MOF-hybrid-assisted strategy" for the preparation of a high-efficiency oxygen evolution catalyst. MOF-derived PBAs were successfully synthesized via an anion-exchange method using bimetallic Co/Mn-ZIF as a template. The composite electrocatalyst possesses abundant hierarchical porosity, a core-shell morphology, and a large specific surface area. Most significantly, the in situ transformed amorphous hydroxide in the alkaline medium water oxidation process can serve as an enhanced active component for structural benefits. This leads to excellent OER electrocatalytic activity and stability versus a state-of-the-art $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ -based benchmark. This MOF-hybrid-assisted strategy offers new insights into the design and development of non-calcined composites for electrocatalysts.
